<div class="faq-item">

    <div class="faq-header" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">

        <div class="faq-title">
            Q.1 Why should DbContext not be Singleton in ASP.NET Core?
        </div>

        <div class="faq-action">

            <div class="view-btn">
                View Answer
            </div>

            <div class="arrow">
                ▼
            </div>

        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="faq-answer">

        <h2>What is DbContext?</h2>

        <p>
            DbContext is the main class used to communicate with the database
            in Entity Framework Core.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is responsible for:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Executing database queries</li>
            <li>Tracking entity changes</li>
            <li>Saving data into database</li>
            <li>Managing database connections</li>
        </ul>

        <hr />

        <h2>Incorrect Registration</h2>

        <p>
            Registering DbContext as Singleton is considered a bad practice.
        </p>

        <pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ApplicationDbContext>();
</code>
</pre>

        <p>
            Singleton creates only one object for the entire application lifetime.
        </p>

        <p>
            This means:
        </p>

        <pre>
<code>
All users share the SAME DbContext instance.
</code>
</pre>

        <hr />

        <h2>Why is this Dangerous?</h2>

        <p>
            DbContext is NOT thread-safe.
        </p>

        <p>
            Multiple requests using the same DbContext object
            can create serious application issues.
        </p>

        <p>
            Problems may include:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Concurrency issues</li>
            <li>Incorrect data updates</li>
            <li>Tracking conflicts</li>
            <li>Memory leaks</li>
            <li>Unexpected application behavior</li>
        </ul>

        <hr />

        <h2>Real World Example</h2>

        <p>
            Suppose two users are using the application simultaneously:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>User A updates student data</li>
            <li>User B deletes the same student data</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Since both requests are sharing the same DbContext instance,
            entity tracking may conflict internally.
        </p>

        <p>
            This can result in:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Wrong data being saved</li>
            <li>Concurrency exceptions</li>
            <li>Application crashes</li>
        </ul>

        <hr />

        <h2>Change Tracking Problem</h2>

        <p>
            DbContext internally tracks entities and their states.
        </p>

        <p>
            Example:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Added</li>
            <li>Modified</li>
            <li>Deleted</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            If DbContext is Singleton,
            old tracked entities remain in memory for a long time.
        </p>

        <p>
            This increases memory usage and may cause stale data issues.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>Correct Approach</h2>

        <p>
            DbContext should always be registered as Scoped.
        </p>

        <pre>
<code>
builder.Services.AddScoped<ApplicationDbContext>();
</code>
</pre>

        <p>
            Scoped lifetime creates:
        </p>

        <pre>
<code>
One DbContext instance per HTTP request
</code>
</pre>

        <p>
            This keeps each request isolated and safe.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <h2>Request Flow with Scoped DbContext</h2>

        <pre>
<code>
Request Starts
      ↓
DI Container Creates DbContext
      ↓
Repository Uses DbContext
      ↓
Request Completes
      ↓
DbContext Gets Disposed
</code>
</pre>

        <hr />

        <h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>DbContext is not thread-safe</li>
            <li>DbContext should never be Singleton</li>
            <li>Scoped lifetime is recommended for DbContext</li>
            <li>Each request should get separate DbContext instance</li>
            <li>Singleton DbContext can cause concurrency and memory issues</li>
        </ul>

        <hr />

        <h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
            DbContext should always use Scoped lifetime because
            it maintains internal tracking state and should not be shared
            across multiple users and requests.
        </p>

    </div>

</div>